WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.919 --> 00:00:02.050
Devil Fruit!

00:00:02.050 --> 00:00:03.050
Ax hands!

00:00:03.050 --> 00:00:05.576
And a heartbreaking moment pulled straight
from the manga.

00:00:05.576 --> 00:00:10.470
Netflix recently dropped the official trailer
for "One Piece," and fans like what they see.

00:00:10.470 --> 00:00:12.695
Some spoilers ahead.

00:00:12.695 --> 00:00:16.880
The "One Piece" trailer kicks off in an epic
fashion, depicting the incredibly influential

00:00:16.880 --> 00:00:19.120
public execution of Gol D. Roger.

00:00:19.120 --> 00:00:24.622
In "One Piece," Gol D. Roger is the most legendary
pirate in history and the captain of the Roger Pirates.

00:00:24.622 --> 00:00:28.990
He became the Pirate King by conquering the
Grand Line and obtaining the One Piece.

00:00:28.990 --> 00:00:32.660
While his accomplishments on the seas made
him a legend, they also put him directly in

00:00:32.660 --> 00:00:34.600
the World Government's crosshairs.

00:00:34.600 --> 00:00:38.980
Dying of an incurable disease, Gol D. Roger
turned himself in, and the government planned

00:00:38.980 --> 00:00:42.079
to execute him publicly as a message to all
pirates.

00:00:42.079 --> 00:00:47.059
Instead, with his last words, Roger refused
to reveal the location of the One Piece, inspiring

00:00:47.059 --> 00:00:51.430
a new generation of pirates, including Luffy,
to search for the hidden treasure and claim

00:00:51.430 --> 00:00:55.517
his title of King of the Pirates, ushering
in the Golden Age of Pirates.

00:00:55.517 --> 00:00:59.760
From the looks of it, Netflix's "One Piece"
sticks close to the source material for Roger's

00:00:59.760 --> 00:01:04.060
execution, with practically a shot-for-shot
adaptation of the same scene from the anime.

00:01:04.060 --> 00:01:08.180
With that in mind, chances are the live-action
series gives us a glimpse at some of the Roger

00:01:08.180 --> 00:01:12.720
Pirates, as both Shanks and Buggy attended
their captain's execution, and are set to

00:01:12.720 --> 00:01:14.920
appear in "One Piece."

00:01:14.920 --> 00:01:18.850
Speaking of Shanks, the "One Piece" trailer
also gives fans their first look at the fan-favorite

00:01:18.850 --> 00:01:23.009
character in live-action, including his heroics
that not only set Luffy on the trajectory

00:01:23.009 --> 00:01:26.512
of becoming a pirate, but is one of the series'
most iconic moments.

00:01:26.512 --> 00:01:30.150
In the trailer, we get a quick glimpse of
Shanks and Luffy on a small boat as a giant

00:01:30.150 --> 00:01:31.897
monster erupts from the sea.

00:01:31.897 --> 00:01:35.790
While it may not look like much to the average
viewer, "One Piece" fans will quickly recognize

00:01:35.790 --> 00:01:40.409
this as a defining moment in Luffy's story,
where Shanks sacrifices his arm to save the

00:01:40.409 --> 00:01:41.649
boy from a Sea King.

00:01:41.649 --> 00:01:45.000
"No big deal, it's just an arm, Luffy."

00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:49.100
Early in "One Piece," a young Luffy finds
himself thrown into the ocean, unable to swim

00:01:49.100 --> 00:01:53.054
because of his Devil Fruit powers, with a
massive Sea King ready to eat him.

00:01:53.054 --> 00:01:58.280
Just as the monster goes in for a bite, Shanks
saves Luffy, sacrificing his left arm to ensure

00:01:58.280 --> 00:01:59.280
the boy survives.

00:01:59.280 --> 00:02:04.820
Shortly after, Shanks and his Red Hair Pirates
depart, leaving Luffy in Foosha Village, but

00:02:04.820 --> 00:02:09.429
not before the captain gives him his iconic
straw hat, inspiring Luffy to become a greater

00:02:09.429 --> 00:02:11.520
pirate than Shanks.

00:02:11.520 --> 00:02:13.110
Of course, the biggest draw of "One Piece"

00:02:13.110 --> 00:02:17.480
is seeing all the beloved characters brought
to live-action for the first time, and Netflix's

00:02:17.480 --> 00:02:19.989
trailer features plenty of heroes and villains.

00:02:19.989 --> 00:02:26.590
The Straw Hat Crew is front and center in
the trailer, with Luffy, Zoro, Nami, Usopp,

00:02:26.590 --> 00:02:31.459
and Sanji looking as if the fan-favorite pirates
walked directly off the pages of Eiichiro

00:02:31.459 --> 00:02:32.780
Oda's manga.

00:02:32.780 --> 00:02:37.269
We even see the crew wearing a few different
outfits, further showcasing how closely "One

00:02:37.269 --> 00:02:39.224
Piece" is sticking to the source material.

00:02:39.224 --> 00:02:44.050
Outside of the Straw Hats, the trailer shows
Marine vice admiral Monkey D. Garp in attendance

00:02:44.050 --> 00:02:45.800
at Gol D. Roger's execution.

00:02:45.800 --> 00:02:51.350
We also see Alvida, the captain of the Alvida
Pirates, and her ship, the Miss Love Duck.

00:02:51.350 --> 00:02:55.670
We even get a quick look at Dracule Mihawk,
one of the Seven Warlords of the Sea and a

00:02:55.670 --> 00:02:58.750
large part of Zoro's early story.

00:02:58.750 --> 00:03:02.440
What makes Netflix's "One Piece" look like
such a promising live-action adaptation is

00:03:02.440 --> 00:03:06.640
that the production didn't heavily rely on
visual effects to bring the world to life,

00:03:06.640 --> 00:03:10.610
building massive sets instead, and the trailer
shows them off in all their glory.

00:03:10.610 --> 00:03:14.630
From the beginning, we see Loguetown, where
Gol D. Roger was born and executed.

00:03:14.630 --> 00:03:19.312
The production even built the tall execution
platform, where "One Piece" Season 1 could end.

00:03:19.312 --> 00:03:23.690
Later in the trailer, Baratie is in full view,
where a few important battles will go down

00:03:23.690 --> 00:03:27.356
before Luffy, Zoro, and Nami add Sanji to
the crew.

00:03:27.356 --> 00:03:31.400
Throughout the trailer, we get glimpses of
other iconic "One Piece" locations, and the

00:03:31.400 --> 00:03:36.689
production also built multiple pirate ships
to film on, like the Going Merry and the Miss Love Duck.

00:03:36.689 --> 00:03:40.360
While it may not seem like a massive feat,
Netflix could've easily used green screens

00:03:40.360 --> 00:03:44.450
and visual effects when filming "One Piece,"
and it's almost surprising that they didn't,

00:03:44.450 --> 00:03:46.830
given Hollywood's current reliance on visual
effects.

00:03:46.830 --> 00:03:51.470
Instead, they had crews build them on-location,
and from the trailer, it seems that investment

00:03:51.470 --> 00:03:56.218
paid off, as Eiichiro Oda's beautiful world
looks incredible.

00:03:56.218 --> 00:04:00.150
While Netflix relied on practical effects
to bring the locations of "One Piece" to life,

00:04:00.150 --> 00:04:04.157
that isn't the case for the Devil Fruit powers,
which are also on full display.

00:04:04.157 --> 00:04:08.270
Luffy's powers come from the gum gum fruit,
which he ate as a child, giving his entire

00:04:08.270 --> 00:04:09.915
body the properties of rubber.

00:04:09.915 --> 00:04:15.456
With it, Luffy can stretch, bounce, bend,
twist, and inflate any part of his body, with

00:04:15.456 --> 00:04:18.600
even crazier techniques coming later in the
series.

00:04:18.600 --> 00:04:22.890
Netflix's trailer holds out most of Luffy's
abilities until the end, showcasing them during

00:04:22.890 --> 00:04:24.360
his battle against Arlong.

00:04:24.360 --> 00:04:29.342
We see him stretch his mouth, legs, and arms,
showing off one of his signature moves

00:04:29.342 --> 00:04:33.670
Similarly, the "One Piece" trailer also gives
our first look at Buggy's chop chop fruit,

00:04:33.670 --> 00:04:37.299
which lets him split his body into pieces,
reassembling them at will.

00:04:37.299 --> 00:04:41.400
"Chop Chop cannoooooon!"

00:04:41.400 --> 00:04:44.990
Although the trailer only showcases Luffy
and Buggy's Devil Fruit abilities, there's

00:04:44.990 --> 00:04:48.373
a chance "One Piece" could feature another
Devil Fruit user.

00:04:48.373 --> 00:04:52.190
We don't know exactly how much Season 1 of
the Netflix series will cover, but assuming

00:04:52.190 --> 00:04:56.660
it wraps up the East Blue Saga, it could end
with the introduction of Captain Smoker, a

00:04:56.660 --> 00:04:58.130
ruthless Marine with smoke powers.

00:04:58.130 --> 00:05:03.760
Either way, Luffy and the Straw Hat crew will
have their hands full with the show's confirmed villains.

00:05:03.760 --> 00:05:07.430
Axe-Hand Morgan doesn't outright appear in
the "One Piece" trailer, but Netflix sure

00:05:07.430 --> 00:05:09.638
does a lot to tease his imposing presence.

00:05:09.638 --> 00:05:12.699
In one shot, just an ax and a hand take up
the entire screen.

00:05:12.699 --> 00:05:16.850
While general audiences may not look twice
at the shot, fans of the anime will instantly

00:05:16.850 --> 00:05:18.160
understand the reference.

00:05:18.160 --> 00:05:22.500
Then, just as Luffy is gearing up to unleash
a massive stretchy kick, eagle-eyed fans will

00:05:22.500 --> 00:05:25.059
spot a poster of Morgan behind the pirate
captain.

00:05:25.059 --> 00:05:28.810
It's difficult to determine exactly what the
former Marine Captain looks like, but his

00:05:28.810 --> 00:05:30.680
metal jaw stands out very clearly.

00:05:30.680 --> 00:05:34.710
"One Piece" fans know that Axe-Hand Morgan
is Luffy's first adversary that challenges

00:05:34.710 --> 00:05:36.550
his plans of becoming the Pirate King.

00:05:36.550 --> 00:05:41.760
As the main antagonist of the first arc of
"One Piece," Morgan abuses his military power,

00:05:41.760 --> 00:05:43.688
ruling Shells Town with an iron fist.

00:05:43.688 --> 00:05:48.154
Or at least, an ax fist…you get it — he
has an ax for a hand.

00:05:48.154 --> 00:05:52.060
Although Morgan is a worthy adversary for
a young, inexperienced Luffy, he won't be

00:05:52.060 --> 00:05:56.130
the Straw Hat captain's biggest challenge
in Netflix's "One Piece."

00:05:56.130 --> 00:06:00.180
Unlike Axe-Hand Morgan, Arlong shows his shark
face throughout the "One Piece" trailer, and

00:06:00.180 --> 00:06:04.412
the fish-man has the same menacing and imposing
presence as he does in the anime.

00:06:04.412 --> 00:06:07.639
"I'll do you the kindness of killing you all
together."

00:06:07.639 --> 00:06:12.080
Without diving too deep into spoilers, Arlong
plays a pivotal role in the Straw Hats' early

00:06:12.080 --> 00:06:17.000
story, particularly regarding Nami, her backstory,
and her official addition to the crew.

00:06:17.000 --> 00:06:21.130
Luffy runs into Arlong after making his way
through Axe-Hand Morgan, Buggy, and the Black

00:06:21.130 --> 00:06:25.280
Cat Pirates, so don't expect to see him until
the end of Season 1.

00:06:25.280 --> 00:06:29.870
However, he'll be worth the wait, as he gives
Luffy an immense obstacle to overcome, bringing

00:06:29.870 --> 00:06:31.860
an interesting fighting style with him.

00:06:31.860 --> 00:06:36.229
A humanoid shark, he primarily attacks with
his long serrated nose, but during his fight

00:06:36.229 --> 00:06:39.000
with Luffy, he pulls out a few crazy moves.

00:06:39.000 --> 00:06:42.860
It will be interesting to see if they make
it to the live-action "One Piece."

00:06:42.860 --> 00:06:46.430
While Arlong will be a massive challenge for
Luffy and the Straw Hats to overcome, there's

00:06:46.430 --> 00:06:50.600
an incredibly emotional moment that comes
during the story arc, one that longtime "One

00:06:50.600 --> 00:06:54.780
Piece" fans will have to emotionally prepare
to re-experience in the live-action series.

00:06:54.780 --> 00:07:02.394
"I'm gonna kick your a** because no one messes
with my friends."

00:07:02.394 --> 00:07:07.190
When Luffy and Zoro meet Nami, she has a very
complicated past, and this moment pulls on

00:07:07.190 --> 00:07:10.660
every emotional heartstring relating to her
tragic backstory.

00:07:10.660 --> 00:07:15.220
The series introduces Nami as a thief, stealing
treasure from other pirates and bringing them

00:07:15.220 --> 00:07:18.977
back to her captain and pirate crew: Arlong
and the Arlong Pirates.

00:07:18.977 --> 00:07:23.470
It's definitely a one-sided relationship,
with Nami feeling trapped rather than enjoying

00:07:23.470 --> 00:07:25.379
her time with the fish-man pirates.

00:07:25.379 --> 00:07:30.310
Eventually, she can no longer take Arlong's
abuse, and in a very emotional moment, Luffy

00:07:30.310 --> 00:07:34.350
places his straw hat on her head, signaling
he'll do whatever it takes for his friend.

00:07:34.350 --> 00:07:38.639
To make it even better, Netflix rips the scene
straight from the manga and anime, proving

00:07:38.639 --> 00:07:42.847
that "One Piece" is sticking close to the
source material for its live-action adaptation.

00:07:42.847 --> 00:07:50.567
"Of course I'll help youuuuu!"

00:07:50.567 --> 00:07:53.770
In the trailer, Luffy and Koby look at a wall
of wanted posters.

00:07:53.770 --> 00:07:58.180
The first and most obvious is Alvida, who
both Luffy and Koby have already met, and

00:07:58.180 --> 00:08:01.889
her wanted poster reveals a 5 million berry
bounty on her head.

00:08:01.889 --> 00:08:07.139
In the next shot, Foxy's wanted poster is
on full display, revealing an impressive 24

00:08:07.139 --> 00:08:10.450
million berry bounty on the captain of the
Foxy Pirates.

00:08:10.450 --> 00:08:12.581
Then, there's Bellamy's wanted poster.

00:08:12.581 --> 00:08:16.909
Bellamy is another antagonist the Straw Hats
will eventually run into, but the earliest

00:08:16.909 --> 00:08:18.729
that could happen is probably Season 2.

00:08:18.729 --> 00:08:22.949
Although it may be a while until these teases
pay off, it's nice that "One Piece" is planning

00:08:22.949 --> 00:08:27.470
ahead, expecting the Netflix series to be
a hit and garner enough attention to return

00:08:27.470 --> 00:08:28.720
for multiple seasons.

00:08:28.720 --> 00:08:33.450
If the trailer is anything to go off of, the
teases will eventually pay off, as the live-action

00:08:33.450 --> 00:08:35.620
"One Piece" looks as promising as ever.

00:08:36.000 --> 00:08:37.580
"Where's my face?"

